= The Classroom in the Void

#chapter_info(
  focus: "Space Exploration, Education, Math/Physics",
  plot: "Mandatory learning during space flight."
)

Location: "The Ark" - Leaving Earth's Orbit.

The launch was cataclysmic. The spacecraft shuddered as the massive propulsion system fought against Earth's gravity. Inside the command module, Dr. Aris felt the overwhelming pressure pinning him to his seat.

"We are approaching the escape velocity!" Leon shouted over the roar. "Prepare for zero-G!"

Suddenly, the shaking stopped. A pencil began to float in the air. Through the reinforced window, the curve of the Earth was visible—a glowing blue sphere against the infinite darkness of the cosmos. They had entered orbit.

"Beautiful, isn't it?" Leon unbuckled his seatbelt. "The universe is our new playground. But Aris, to survive in this interstellar environment, you are currently an idiot."

Aris blinked. "Excuse me? I have a PhD (Doctor) in Geology!"

"That’s terrestrial thinking," Leon scoffed. "Up here, you are a novice, a complete fresher. Welcome to Husk University. I am your principal, headmaster, and mentor. You are the only pupil."

Leon floated over to a high-tech screen and pulled up a digital timetable. "This is your new curriculum. It’s compulsory. No truancy allowed."

Aris looked at the schedule. It was a nightmare.

08:00 - Quantum Mechanics & Particle Physics

10:00 - Astronomy: Mapping the Galaxy

12:00 - Calculus & Geometry: Calculating Trajectories

14:00 - Alien Psychology (Optional Elective)

"I have to learn calculus again?" Aris groaned. "I hated maths in primary school."

"It's a prerequisite for survival!" Leon insisted. "If you can't solve a simple equation about the radius of a meteorite or the density of a comet, we will crash! We need to calculate the dimension and mass of every chunk of space debris to avoid a collision."

Leon handed Aris a heavy tablet. "Here is your textbook. I expect you to acquire this knowledge by tomorrow. You will have to submit a thesis on the composition of Martian dust. And don't try to plagiarise from the ship's database. I have software to detect cheating. If you fail, I will suspend your food rations."

"This is abuse!" Aris argued. "I can't cram all this information in one day. My brain can't handle this intensity."

"Be diligent, Aris!" Leon floated upside down. "Use your intelligence. We are on a scientific expedition. You need to master the principles of aerodynamics and thermodynamics. Look at the spectrum of that star over there. Is it emitting ultraviolet or radioactive waves? You need to analyse the signal from our antenna."

Aris sighed and looked at the diagram of a circuit board Leon wanted him to fix. "What happens if I pass?"

"Then I will grant you a degree," Leon smiled. "A Master of Mars. And maybe a scholarship—which means I won't charge you a fee for the oxygen you breathe."

"You were going to charge me?"

"Of course. Nothing is free, not even in a vacuum. Now, stop complaining and start your assignment. The deadline is when we pass the Moon."

Aris floated to the corner, clutching his tablet. He opened the first chapter: "How to fix a fusion reactor with a spoon." It was going to be a long semester
