= Culture Shock in a Can

#chapter_info(
  focus: "Culture & History, Language, Social Roles",
  plot: "Leon tries to design a future Martian civilization."
)

**Location:** The Recreation Room (now converted into a "Throne Room").

Three weeks into the **voyage**, cabin fever had set in. Aris walked into the mess hall to find Leon sitting on a pile of boxes, wearing a makeshift crown made of **wire**.

"Halt, **commoner**!" Leon commanded. "You are entering the **sacred** hall of the New Martian **Empire**. Show some **respect**."

Aris sighed. "Leon, it's just the cafeteria. And why are you sitting like that?"

"I am establishing a new **civilization**," Leon declared, gesturing with a plastic spoon like it was a royal scepter. "We cannot rely on Earth's outdated **traditions** or **customs**. We need a cultural **renaissance**. I am the **emperor**, the **monarch** of this metal box. You, Aris, are my loyal **subject**, or perhaps my **servant**... no, let's say **prime minister**."

"I prefer **colleague**," Aris muttered.

"Silence!" Leon stood up. "In this **hierarchy**, I have the highest **status** and **prestige**. We are returning to a form of enlightened **feudalism**. I am the **lord**, and you are the... well, you do the work, so you are the **peasant**. But a well-paid one!"

"This sounds like **slavery** with extra steps," Aris argued. "And what about our **heritage**? We are ambassadors of Earth. We represent democracy and **liberty**."

"Democracy is slow," Leon waved his hand. "My **regime** is efficient. Now, let's discuss **etiquette**. From now on, whenever you approach me, you must perform a specific **ritual**. You must **bow** three times and offer a **tribute**—preferably a coffee."

Aris rolled his eyes. "This is a **cult**, not a culture."

"Speaking of culture," Leon continued, ignoring him. "I have decided that English is too inefficient. We need a new **language** to **unite** our future Martian colony. I call it 'Leonish'."

Leon walked to a whiteboard covered in strange drawings. "Look at these **symbols**. I have replaced the **alphabet** with **pictographs** based on my face. This smiling face means 'Good morning', and this angry face means 'The engine is on fire'."

"That's not a language; that's just emojis," Aris critiqued. "A real language needs complex **grammar**, **syntax**, and a rich **vocabulary**. It evolves through **usage** over centuries. You can't just invent a **dialect** overnight."

"Watch me," Leon grinned. "In Leonish, we don't use **verbs**. We use **gestures**. It's all about **non-verbal** communication. For example, wiggling your **thumb** means 'I agree'."

"And what about **pronunciation**?" Aris asked. "Or **intonation**?"

"We speak in a high-pitched **accent** to simulate the thin atmosphere of Mars," Leon demonstrated, squeaking like a mouse. "It distinguishes the **elite** from the **vulgar** masses."

Aris rubbed his temples. "Leon, if we meet **aliens**, and you speak to them like that, they will think we are **lunatics** (insane). They won't see us as a **superior** species; they'll see us as a snack."

"Nonsense," Leon sat back on his throne. "Future **generations** will look back at this moment as the **origin** of a great **dynasty**. My **descendants**—or rather, my clones—will worship me as a **legend**, a **myth**."

"Right now," Aris said, grabbing a packet of dried food. "The only **myth** is your sanity. I'm going to eat my lunch. In **private**."

"Wait!" Leon shouted. "You forgot the **ritual**! And you didn't wiggle your **thumb**!"

Aris walked out, wondering if **mutiny** (rebellion) was a viable option in space.

