= Kitchen Nightmares

#chapter_info(
  focus: "Food & Health, Body & Mind",
  plot: "Food synthesizer failure. Sickness ensues."
)

**Location:** The Galley (Kitchen).

The air in the kitchen smelled of burnt **toast** and melted **plastic**. The ChefBot 3000 was sputtering, its **lid** flapping open and closed like a hungry bird.

"I am **starving**," Leon announced, tapping his **cutlery** (a **fork** and a **spoon**) against an empty **plate**. "I demand a **banquet**. I want a three-course **meal** with **appetizers**, a main **dish**, and a **dessert**."

Aris looked at the smoking machine. "Leon, the **recipe** database is corrupted. The machine doesn't know the difference between a **tomato** and a **potato**. It might **poison** us."

"Nonsense," Leon scoffed. "I am a **gourmet**. I have a refined **palate**. I can appreciate any **cuisine**, even experimental ones. ChefBot, **serve** dinner!"

The machine whirred. It didn't **bake**, **fry**, or **roast**. It just... _extruded_.

_SPLAT._

A pile of grey sludge landed in a **bowl**.

"Ah," Leon peered at it. "It looks like... **porridge**? Or maybe mashed **eggplant**?"

Aris sniffed it. "It smells like **vinegar** mixed with old **socks**."

"Don't be picky," Leon said, grabbing a **knife**. He sliced a piece of something that looked like a **sausage** but smelled like **vanilla** **ice cream**. "This is fusion food! Look, here is a **sandwich** made of **beef**, **strawberry** **jam**, and... is that **mustard**?"

Leon took a big bite. He **chewed** vigorously. He **swallowed**. His face turned green.

"Interesting **flavor**," Leon wheezed, clutching his **throat**. "It's a bit... **sour**. And **bitter**. And... oh god."

"Don't eat it!" Aris yelled, but he was too hungry. He grabbed a **biscuit** that looked safe. It turned out to be a block of compressed **salt** and **pepper**. He choked, grabbing a **mug** of **beverage** to wash it down. It was hot **ketchup** mixed with **coffee**.

Ten minutes later, the **buffet** was over, and the **suffering** began.

Aris lay on the floor, holding his **stomach** (abdomen). "I think I have **food poisoning**."

"Me too," Leon groaned, lying on the **bench**. "My **abdomen** feels like it's exploding. I feel **nauseous** (sick)."

Aris tried to check his own **symptoms**. "My **headache** is splitting. I feel **dizzy**. My **vision** is blurry. Leon, check my **pulse**."

Leon crawled over, his hand shaking. He touched Aris's **wrist**. "Your **heartbeat** is irregular. You have a **fever**. Your **forehead** is burning hot. You are definitely **ill**."

"My **intestines** are twisting," Aris moaned. "I think my **liver** and **kidneys** are shutting down. That 'sausage' was toxic waste!"

"We need **medical** attention," Leon said, trying to stand up but failing due to **fatigue**. "I am the ship's **physician** now. Where is the **first aid kit**?"

He dragged a box over. It contained random items: a box of **aspirin** (pills), a bottle of **cough syrup**, some **bandages**, and a large **syringe** (for injections).

"Okay," Leon said, putting on a **mask**. "I will **diagnose** you. You have Acute Space Indigestion. The **remedy** is... everything in this box."

"No!" Aris shouted weakly. "Just give me some water to flush out the **toxins**. I need to **digest** this mess."

"I prescribe a strict **diet**," Leon muttered, swallowing a **pill** himself. "From now on, we are **vegetarians**. No... **fruitarians**. We only eat **apples** and **pears**. No more **dairy**, no **cheese**, no **butter**, and definitely no experimental **seafood**."

"We don't have apples," Aris reminded him. "We only have those mutant **cabbages** in the greenhouse."

"Then we eat cabbage soup," Leon sighed, closing his eyes. "For the rest of the **voyage**. It's vital for our **fitness** and **hygiene**."

Aris curled up in pain. "If I **survive** this, I'm going to sue you for **malpractice**."

"Rest now," Leon whispered, drifting into a **coma**\-like sleep. "Tomorrow, we start our **recovery**. Or we die. Either way, no more cooking."
