= Grand Finale

#chapter_info(
  focus: "Review of difficult words",
  plot: "Wrapping up the story."
)
**Location:** The Terrace of the Musk Mansion (at sunrise).

The Martian sun peeked over the **horizon**, casting a long **shadow** across the red **plain**. The **atmosphere** was tense but hopeful. Leon stood on the **balcony**, holding his "shovel-guitar." Aris stood beside him, holding a **flag** made of a white bedsheet—a symbol of **peace** and **surrender**.

"Are you ready?" Aris asked.

"I was born ready," Leon adjusted his tattered **collar**. "This will be my greatest **performance**."

Below them, the army of mutant animals waited. The Lio-sheep looked up, its expression **stern** but **curious**. Gary the salamander was chewing on a piece of **cable**.

Leon took a deep breath and struck a chord. _TWANG!_

It wasn't perfect, but it was... **musical**. He began to play a slow, **gentle** **melody**. It wasn't the chaotic noise from before. It was a **sincere** tune, full of **emotion**.

"Friends!" Leon sang (surprisingly in **tune**). "Why do we **fight**? Why do we **quarrel**? We are all stuck on this rock together! Let us **unite**!"

The chickens stopped clucking. The Lio-sheep sat down. The **rhythm** seemed to **soothe** their savage **instincts**.

Aris joined in, tapping a **rhythm** on a plastic **bucket**. "We offer you **freedom**! We offer you **respect**! No more **cages**! No more **chains**!"

It worked. The animals didn't **attack**. Instead, the Lio-sheep let out a soft **moan** (a happy sound) and wagged its tail. The **hostility** vanished. The **siege** was over.

"We did it!" Leon whispered, tears of **joy** in his eyes. "We achieved **harmony**!"

Just then, a loud **boom** echoed through the **valley**. A bright light flashed in the sky.

"Is that a **meteorite**?" Aris gasped.

"No," Leon squinted. "It's a **spaceship**."

A sleek, silver vessel descended and **landed** gracefully near their wrecked shuttle. The door opened, and a **delegation** of humans in pristine white **uniforms** walked out.

"Dr. Aris? Mr. Husk?" the leader of the group asked. "I am Captain Nova from the Earth Rescue **Mission**. We received your **distress** signal... three months ago. Sorry for the **delay**. Traffic was terrible near the Moon."

Leon dropped his shovel. "Rescue? We are saved!"

"We are here to take you home," Captain Nova said, looking around at the ruined mansion, the mutant chickens, and the two dirty, bearded men. "Although... it looks like you have established a... unique **community** here."

Leon looked at Aris. Then he looked at the Lio-sheep, who was now nuzzling his leg. He looked at the ugly, half-finished **villa** they had built with their own hands.

"Go home?" Leon hesitated. "Back to the **taxes**, the **paparazzi**, and the boring **board** meetings?"

Aris looked at the red **landscape**. It was harsh, **barren**, and **dangerous**. But it was also theirs. They had survived **disasters**, **diseases**, and **mutinies**. They had created a **language**, a **government** (sort of), and a new **species**.

"Actually," Aris said, surprising himself. "We have a lot of **unfinished** business here. We need to **restore** the greenhouse. We need to **educate** the chickens. We have a **vision** for this planet."

"You mean..." Leon grinned. "We stay?"

"We stay," Aris nodded. "This is our **colony**. Our **legacy**."

Leon turned to Captain Nova. "Thank you, Captain. But we **decline** your offer. However, could you leave us some supplies? Specifically, **chocolate**, **shampoo**, and a real **guitar**?"

Captain Nova looked confused but agreed. The crew unloaded crates of **necessities** and took off, leaving the two pioneers behind.

As the ship disappeared into the **cosmos**, Leon put his arm around Aris's shoulder.

"Well, Mr. Prime Minister," Leon said. "We have a **nation** to build. What's the first item on the **agenda**?"

"First," Aris said, picking up a hammer. "We fix the **toilet**."

"Agreed," Leon laughed. "And then... we hold an **election**."

And so, on the dusty plains of Mars, under the **eternal** gaze of the stars, the absurd adventure of Leon and Aris continued. They were **lonely**, they were **crazy**, but they were **free**.

**THE END.**